  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 4,
    "lesson_title": "Impulse Response",
    "lesson_core_move": "Determine an LTI system's impulse response $h(t)$ by evaluating its output to $\\delta$(t) and interpreting that output as the kernel that governs shifted, scaled, and decomposed impulse inputs.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Impulse Response",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Impulse Response",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For an LTI system, the unit impulse is the canonical probe input. In this section, the only input we need is the unshifted impulse at the origin.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\delta(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The direct definition is this: when the input to the system is $\\delta(t)$, the output is called the impulse response and is written as $h(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{\\delta(t)\\}=h(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The input and the response play different roles. The signal $\\delta(t)$ is what enters the system; the signal $h(t)$ is what the system produces in response to that input.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=h(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So, for the base impulse input, identifying the output is the whole move: the output to $\\delta(t)$ is $h(t)$. Later sections will use this same $h(t)$ as the reference response for shifted, scaled, and decomposed impulse inputs.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Impulse Response",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Finding $h(t)$ from a Given $\\Delta$-Input Output",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "An LTI system has output $y(t)=e^{-2t}u(t)$ when the input is $x(t)=\\delta(t)$. Find $h(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the stated input. Since the input is exactly the unit impulse, this is the base case that defines the impulse response.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\delta(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For an LTI system, the output produced by $\\delta(t)$ is named $h(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{\\delta(t)\\}=h(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The problem tells us that the output for this same input is $y(t)=e^{-2t}u(t)$. Therefore that output expression is the impulse response.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "h(t)=e^{-2t}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the system's impulse response is $e^{-2t}u(t)$. No shifting or scaling rule is needed here because the input is the unshifted, unscaled impulse.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Impulse Response",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Finding $h(t)$ by Applying a System Rule to $\\delta$(t)",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For the LTI system $y(t)=2x(t)+x(t-1)$, find $h(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the system rule is given, but the output to $\\delta(t)$ is not given directly. To find the impulse response, evaluate the system at the unit impulse input.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "h(t)=T\\{\\delta(t)\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Set the input signal in the rule to $x(t)=\\delta(t)$. Then each occurrence of $x$ becomes the matching impulse expression.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\delta(t),\\qquad x(t-1)=\\delta(t-1)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute those impulse expressions into the system rule.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=2\\delta(t)+\\delta(t-1)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because this is the output produced when the input is $\\delta(t)$, this output is the impulse response.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "h(t)=2\\delta(t)+\\delta(t-1)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the system's impulse response is $2\\delta(t)+\\delta(t-1)$. This example only evaluates the system rule at $\\delta(t)$; it does not use convolution or impulse decomposition.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Impulse Response",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Distinguishing the Impulse Input from the Impulse Response",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "An LTI system has impulse response $h(t)=e^{-t}u(t)$. Identify the impulse input and the impulse response in this statement.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the phrase impulse response. It names the output produced by the unit impulse input, not the input itself.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{\\delta(t)\\}=h(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The impulse input is the probe signal that would be applied to the LTI system.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\text{impulse input}=\\delta(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The impulse response is the output signal named by $h(t)$. In the statement, that response is the function given after $h(t)=$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\text{impulse response}=h(t)=e^{-t}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So $\\delta(t)$ is the impulse input, while $h(t)=e^{-t}u(t)$ is the resulting output response. The common error is to call $\\delta(t)$ the impulse response, but $\\delta(t)$ is the input that defines it.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Impulse Response",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Interpreting $h(t)$ as the Reference Response for Later Impulse Inputs",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "An LTI system has output $y(t)=e^{-t}u(t)$ when $x(t)=\\delta(t)$. Identify the reference response that later shifted or scaled impulse inputs will modify.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the given output is produced when the input is the unit impulse. That means the output is the impulse response.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\delta(t)\\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad y(t)=e^{-t}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "By definition, the output to $\\delta(t)$ is named $h(t)$. So the given output expression becomes the system's impulse response.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "h(t)=e^{-t}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This $h(t)$ is the reference response for the system. Later shifted, scaled, and decomposed impulse inputs will reuse modified copies of this same response pattern.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the reference response is $h(t)=e^{-t}u(t)$. In this section, the task is only to name that response and state its role, not to compute any shifted or scaled outputs yet.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
